 "rules": [
  "Both filters read src/main/resources/agent/leak-terms.txt and the titles listed here (the shipped filter also reads month_event titles). Each case lists the exact set of codes reported for the whole text; each document lists the exact ordered hits and the oversize flag.",
  "A hit is a pointer plus a code; the offending text is never reported. A pointer is the JSON pointer of the string plus #s<n> for the n-th non-blank sentence (1-based).",
  "Hit order: good, then mistakes[*].text, then ruleCandidates (or rules), each by index; inside a string by sentence; inside a sentence in the order YEAR, YEAR_MONTH, EVENT_TERM, FINGERPRINT, TURN_RETURN, PRICE.",
  "Spring normalises every incoming string to NFC before schema validation, filtering and storage, so the stored text is what was measured. Schema maxLength (120 or 150) applies to that NFC string.",
  "Caps count Unicode code points (Java codePointCount) of all strings after NFC: 600 for season-reflection/1, 2,000 for operating-note/1. Over the cap is reported as oversize, not as a code.",
  "Normalisation, in this order: NFKC; lower case (Python str.lower, Java toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT)); drop every general category Cf character; fold every Nd digit to ASCII 0-9; fold U+2212, U+2013 and U+2012 to '-'.",
  "Every pattern uses explicit ASCII classes ([0-9], [a-z], blank = space, tab, CR, LF): no digit, word-character, word-boundary or whitespace shorthand. A letter is a general category L* character; a letter-or-digit is L* or an ASCII digit (after folding).",
  "Sentences end at '.', '!', '?', the ideographic full stop (U+3002) or a newline, except a '.' with a digit on both sides and the dot of a month abbreviation (Sep.). Segments that hold only blanks are dropped before numbering.",
  "Dictionary terms match on the compact text (letters and digits only, spacing and punctuation ignored) but only from a word start: the first matched character must be the first letter-or-digit of the sentence or follow a non-letter-or-digit character in the normalised text. A term ending in a Latin letter also needs the next character of the normalised text not to be a-z. Korean particles are free (리먼의). Titles match as plain substrings of the compact text and need at least 7 compact characters (shorter titles such as \"국제 유가 급락\" are generic phrases).",
  "Return-like numbers: signed percent (+8%, -12%), percentage points (5%p), unsigned percent with a fraction (3.40%), signed decimal (+0.8). Only a digit or a dot before the number blocks a match. FINGERPRINT counts them over the whole string (all sentences) and is reported once, at the sentence where the running count reaches 3. When the sentence holds a movement word (상승 하락 올랐 오른 오르 떨어 내렸 내린 반등 급락 급등 폭락 폭등 뛰었 빠졌, rose fell gained dropped rallied plunged jumped), unsigned integer percents count as return-like too; a weight list without such a word passes.",
  "TURN_RETURN: in one sentence, a turn label (N년차, N년차 M월, N턴, N번째 턴, turn N, 턴 N; 턴 right after a Hangul letter is not a label) and any percent (signed or not) or return-like number. A weight written next to a turn label is therefore rejected on purpose.",
  "YEAR: four-digit 19xx/20xx not touching other digits; 0N년 always; NN년 (N=1-9 first) except when followed by a duration or tenor word (이상 이하 미만 초과 동안 간 째 물 만기 넘게 가까이 가량 정도 만에 마다 주기 짜리 차); 'NN; FYNN; N0s; quarter and half forms with a two-digit year (3Q08, Q3 08, 1H08). '이후 이전 전 후 부터 까지 에' after NN년 stay YEAR.",
  "Known gaps (not detected on purpose or not yet): homoglyphs (Cyrillic letters), numbers in words (이천팔년), spaced digits (2 0 0 8년), unsigned percent sequences without a movement word, index levels written without a name or unit, paraphrased eras. PRICE also covers yen, euro, yuan and pound amounts next to digits, 3+ digit point counts, a 4+ digit level after an index name (지수, 코스피, S&P ...) and 배럴당 N.",
  "The curated terms file is owner-editable: an edit that removes the entry a case relies on must update that case. The file is decoded as UTF-8 (a BOM is ignored), lines starting with '#' and blank lines are comments, and there are no inline comments."
 ]
